00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:08.030
and 15.9 millimeters thin.

00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:10.280
So if you would like to
learn more about the device,

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:12.270
check out the link in
the video description.

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:14.040
So let's work with that additional data

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:15.410
and insert a new column here.

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:19.040
Let's call that the average price,

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:21.720
because as you can see here,
these are different articles.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.190
You'll see the total purchase price.

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:24.880
And here you see the quantities.

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:26.910
So many of them were purchased,

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:29.610
and now to have the price per unit,

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:33.430
what we want to do is
now just to calculate.

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:37.060
So here we would like to
divide the purchase price

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:39.910
by the quantity, then
you just press enter.

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:42.770
then what you do now, because
you want to have this formula

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:46.100
included in every single row here.

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:47.600
How would you do that?

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.540
And of course, now you could copy this.

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:51.060
And then what some people might do,

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:53.710
what beginners might do is
just to try to highlight this

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:56.370
in a way like this, just with the mouse

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:58.607
and then insert it here.

00:09:58.607 --> 00:10:00.010
But of course, here, once again,

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.680
the problem is that this would
take you lots amount of time,

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:06.000
especially if you're working
with a very long Excel model

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:07.600
with thousands of rows.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:08.850
Though here the problem is also that

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:11.350
you cannot just use the technique
that I showed you earlier

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:13.490
with navigating around with control

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:15.050
and then the arrow buttons

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:19.120
because if you copy this and
then press control + down,

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:21.170
you will end here at the
very bottom of the sheet.

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:26.170
So you see that this is the
row 1,048,000 something, right?

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:28.150
So this is not possible.

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:29.840
And instead, the little
hack that you can do

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:33.800
is that you move with the
arrows, one to the left,

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:36.860
you move here to the last filled column,

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:40.420
and then you move down
with control + arrow down,

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:42.430
and then you're at the bottom of the table

00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:45.870
and then you move with the
arrow signs once to the right.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:48.190
And now you can use the shortcut
that I showed you earlier,

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:50.600
namely control + shift + arrow up

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:52.485
to then have it all highlighted
up until this point,

00:10:52.485 --> 00:10:55.660
up until where we started
here in the row three,

00:10:55.660 --> 00:10:56.493
as you can see.

00:10:56.493 --> 00:11:00.700
And if I now press control +
V, you have it all populated

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:02.710
up until the end of the table.

00:11:02.710 --> 00:11:04.130
And maybe this looked a bit complicated,

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:06.290
but it's actually pretty
easy and straight forward.

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:07.123
Let's just go back and let me show you

00:11:07.123 --> 00:11:09.180
how you would do this quickly, right?

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:11.000
So this was a starting point.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:13.550
We go up and we have it all populated.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.760
So it's like something that
will really go into your blood.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:17.810
It's very straightforward, very easy.

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:19.760
So we just talked about copy paste.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:20.930
The one thing that we would need

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:24.890
very very frequently is pasting values

00:11:24.890 --> 00:11:25.920
because what you see here

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:28.700
is that what was copied
is not really a value,

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:31.650
not really a number, but always a formula.

00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:35.250
And here another little
shortcut that you can use is f2.

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:39.040
So if you press f2 on such a formula,

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:40.840
it highlights all the cells

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:42.680
that are involved in a formula, right?

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:44.320
So we see that here, the one cell

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:46.120
was divided by the other cell,

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:48.330
and this is just what we copied over.

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:50.100
By the way if you press escape,

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:54.100
then you can always leave
this mode here again.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:55.880
But here, the problem is, or
maybe it's not the problem

00:11:55.880 --> 00:11:57.150
often this is exactly desired.

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:59.070
But here indeed, we
copied over the formula,

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:00.240
but not the values.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:01.990
And of course, what
this means is that here,

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:06.720
if this field changes, of course
this value changes as well.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:07.930
But sometimes we don't want that.

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:09.890
Sometimes we don't wanna copy formulas,

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:12.180
but we want to copy specific values,

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:16.200
specific numbers that
then don't change anymore.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:18.980
And here the manual
mouse way to doing that

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:23.160
is to then highlight this,
do them with control + C,

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:25.570
copy this and then here with paste,

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:27.370
there is this drop down menu.

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:29.800
And then here you could
go on paste values.

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:31.710
And then if you would click on that,

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:34.220
you would see that now
these formulas were replaced

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:36.500
by the specific numbers
that you worked with.

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:38.940
So let me teach you how you
would do this with shortcuts.

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:41.800
And indeed first you need to
highlight this and copy it.

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:43.290
And then once it is copied,

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:47.240
you can press control + Alt
+ V and then this pastes

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:48.650
special menu pops up.

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:50.470
And here, there are lots
of things you can do,

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:53.740
but specifically what we wanna
do now is pasting values.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:57.040
And you see that if I
now press V once again,

00:12:57.040 --> 00:12:59.970
here now, the radio
buttons jump to values.

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:02.430
And then if I press enter,

00:13:02.430 --> 00:13:06.270
then I have it all replaced
with the values indeed.

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:08.420
So once more, a bit
more quickly highlight,

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:11.740
control + C + V + V, and it is replaced.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:12.970
This is how you paste values.

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:14.700
Pasting values is for sure something

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:16.220
that you will also use super frequently.

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:18.680
So definitely worth the
time of learning this.

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:21.060
So let me show you one more
thing that is very important

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:23.470
when you work with fixed
references and for that,

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:24.740
let's include here new column.

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:26.130
And let's imagine that we would want

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:28.360
to show the purchase price.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:31.880
So what we see here though now in GBP,

00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:34.460
so Great British Pound thousand.

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:36.160
So we want to have that in thousand.

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:37.650
Let's just imagine as a little helper,

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:41.503
we here include the number
1000 just to have this here.

00:13:41.503 --> 00:13:43.490
Then now, what you would
of course need to do

00:13:43.490 --> 00:13:47.210
is to divide this number by 1000.

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:49.210
And here too, now that will
now make it a little bit easier

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:51.990
for us, we just highlight this field here.

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:55.130
Now, of course, the problem
is, if you just copy this over,

00:13:55.130 --> 00:13:56.040
it doesn't work.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:57.100
Why doesn't it work?

00:13:57.100 --> 00:13:59.930
Well, here and I'm pressing
f2 now, once again,

00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:02.040
you see this, if you copy this down,

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:05.000
this range here moves again,

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:07.470
which is why you now need
to fix the reference here

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:09.770
of this G1 cell.

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:11.860
And if you want to learn
more about references,

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:13.890
about relative and fixed references,

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:16.060
once you've made a complete
video, just covering that,

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:18.300
I will link it somewhere
above here as well.

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:21.210
But here, the other thing that
you can use is to press f4.

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:23.620
And you see now I pressed it once.

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:26.310
And now we have absolute fixed references

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:28.370
for both the column and the row.

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:30.390
And now with f4, you can just toggle

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:32.250
through the individual modes.

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:34.953
Of course, what we want
to do here is to fix it.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:38.450
And because then if we use
this and if we copy it,

00:14:38.450 --> 00:14:41.530
then over just showing the
level that we did even before,

00:14:41.530 --> 00:14:43.310
you see that this is now already copied.

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:45.250
Now I forgot to highlight these stats.

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:46.770
So now let me show you
an additional thing.

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:48.110
And this is very helpful

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:50.470
if you work with very complex formulas,

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:53.730
where sometimes it's
difficult to just really keep

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:55.470
the overview of what actually is going on.

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:56.940
And for demonstration purposes,

00:14:56.940 --> 00:15:01.370
let me show you just a very
random nonsensical formula.

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:03.710
So completely nonsensical,
just to show us now,

00:15:03.710 --> 00:15:05.880
a bit more complex calculation.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:07.810
Now, of course, if maybe you have an error

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:09.180
or maybe something looks weird.

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.640
Maybe you want to know then
what exactly is going on.

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:13.460
If you look at expressions like this,

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:16.160
then what you can do is you
can evaluate the formula,

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:18.600
by just looking what the reside

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:20.607
of individual parts of the formula is.

00:15:20.607 --> 00:15:22.810
And by doing that, you highlight the part

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:23.657
that you want to know.

00:15:23.657 --> 00:15:26.330
And then if you press the f9 button,

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:28.280
it shows you what the number,

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:32.060
what the output is that this
specific element is returning.

00:15:32.060 --> 00:15:34.330
Now, if I press control + Z for returning,

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:35.730
it again shows the formula,

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:37.690
and I can do this with
other elements as well.

00:15:37.690 --> 00:15:40.760
I can for instance, do
this with this D5 field.

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:45.760
So here we know that D5 is
this 1,005,725, if I press f9,

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:49.070
this is exactly the number it gives me.

00:15:49.070 --> 00:15:51.320
I can indeed also evaluate
more complex expressions.

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:54.370
So if I want to, for instance,
know what all this is,

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:57.430
I press f9 and then it
shows me the other number.

00:15:57.430 --> 00:15:59.240
So once again, this is extremely helpful

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:01.830
if you want to debunk
a more complex formula,

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:04.330
especially maybe if a
formula is returning an error

00:16:04.330 --> 00:16:06.840
and you want to know what
specific parts of the formula

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:08.460
are producing this error,

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:10.320
this is something that
you can frequently use.

